// State-variable filter, zero-delay feedback (TPT). Two trapezoidal integrators in the classic SVF loop, solved in
// closed form per sample (A. Simper, "Linear trapezoidal integrated state variable filter", Cytomic 2013; the same
// filter as V. Zavalishin's TPT SVF with k = 2R). Stable and click-free under fast cutoff / Q modulation, including
// audio-rate modulation, which is why EQ Eight and Auto Filter are built on it.
//
//   g = tan(π·cutoff/sr), k = 1/q,  a1 = 1 / (1 + g·(g + k)),  a2 = g·a1,  a3 = g·a2
//   v3 = x − ic2,  v1 = a1·ic1 + a2·v3 (band),  v2 = ic2 + a2·ic1 + a3·v3 (low),  ic1 ← 2·v1 − ic1,  ic2 ← 2·v2 − ic2
//   (computed in the equivalent shallower form of svf_core below)
//
// `svf` gives every response of one filter core as an output: lp, bp (unity gain at the cutoff), hp, notch, peak
// (lp − hp) and allpass, all from the same two states; read as many as you like at no extra cost. The bell and the
// shelves use the gain-dependent coefficients of the RBJ / Cytomic designs, so each of them is its own core:
// `svf(x, cutoff: c, q: q, gain: 6dB).lowshelf` costs one core, and cores whose outputs are not read are removed
// by the compiler. Cost per core: about ten multiply/adds per sample; the coefficients run once per block unless
// cutoff, q or gain are modulated.
patch svf {
  in    x      : audio
  param cutoff = 1kHz   [20Hz..20kHz] @unit(Hz) @curve(exp) @label("Cutoff")
  param q      = 0.7071 [0.1..40]     @curve(exp) @label("Q")
  param gain   = 0dB    [-24dB..24dB] @unit(dB) @label("Gain")   // bell and shelves only
  out   lp        : audio
  out   bp        : audio
  out   hp        : audio
  out   notch     : audio
  out   peak      : audio
  out   allpass   : audio
  out   bell      : audio
  out   lowshelf  : audio
  out   highshelf : audio

  // The cutoff is kept in [0, 0.49·sr] (tan has its pole at Nyquist); q > 0.
  g = tan(3.141592653589793 * clip(cutoff, 0, 0.49 * sr) / sr)
  k = 1 / max(q, 0.01)
  A = sqrt(gain)              // 10^(dB/40): `gain` is already linear (dB literals are amplitudes)

  f = svf_core(x, g: g, k: k)
  lp      = f.low
  bp      = k * f.band
  hp      = x - k * f.band - f.low
  notch   = x - k * f.band
  peak    = x - k * f.band - 2 * f.low
  allpass = x - 2 * k * f.band

  // RBJ peaking EQ (symmetric boost/cut): k scaled by 1/A.
  b = svf_core(x, g: g, k: k / A)
  bell = x + k / A * (gain - 1) * b.band

  // Shelves: the cutoff moved by A^(∓1/2) so it marks the middle of the transition (in dB).
  ls = svf_core(x, g: g / sqrt(A), k: k)
  lowshelf = x + k * (A - 1) * ls.band + (gain - 1) * ls.low
  hs = svf_core(x, g: g * sqrt(A), k: k)
  highshelf = gain * x + k * (1 - A) * A * hs.band + (1 - gain) * hs.low
}

// The SVF loop itself, for designs that compute their own g and k (g = tan(π·fc/sr) prewarped integrator gain,
// k = 1/Q damping). Outputs the band-pass (peak gain 1/k) and low-pass integrator outputs; every other response
// is a mix of these and the input.
//
// The state update is Simper's ic ← 2·v − ic solved for the new states (T4.10, same filter in exact arithmetic):
//   v = x − ic2,  ic1 ← ic1 + d1·ic1 + b1·v,  ic2 ← ic2 + b1·ic1 + b2·v
//   d1 = 2·a1 − 2 = −2·g·(g + k)·a1,  b1 = 2·a2 = 2·g·a1,  b2 = 2·a3 = b1·g,  band = (ic1 + ic1')/2, low = (ic2 + ic2')/2
// Each new state is two multiply-adds deep from the old ones (the classic form is four), so a chain of filters
// that runs sample by sample (a loop per filter, or an EQ's cascade) is twice as fast; the increments are added to
// the states, which keeps the low-frequency precision of the original (no 1 − 2·a3 near 1).
patch svf_core {
  in    x : audio
  param g = 0.0655  [0..100]   @label("g")   // tan(π · 1 kHz / 48 kHz)
  param k = 1.4142  [0.001..100] @label("k")
  out   band : audio
  out   low  : audio

  a1 = 1 / (1 + g * (g + k))
  d1 = -2 * g * (g + k) * a1
  b1 = 2 * g * a1
  b2 = b1 * g

  v   = x - ic2
  n1  = b1 * v + (d1 * ic1 + ic1)
  n2  = b2 * v + (b1 * ic1 + ic2)
  ic1 = z1(n1)                // integrator states (trapezoidal)
  ic2 = z1(n2)

  band = 0.5 * (ic1 + n1)
  low  = 0.5 * (ic2 + n2)
}
